ants no exemption (the repo carries many), so a leaf that writes junk into a\n793- * quiet sibling phase folder still fails the iteration exactly as before.\n794- */\n795-async function discoverForeignLiveRunDirs({ specFolder, baseArtifactDir }) {\n--\n3881:    const sessionId = readStoredLineageSessionId(loopType, lineageDir) || `fanout-${lineage.label}-${runId}`;\n3882-    const promptLineageDir = path.resolve(process.cwd(), lineageDir);\n3883-    const prompt = buildLoopPrompt(loopType, specFolder, promptLineageDir, sessionId, lineage, researchTopic, {\n3884-      convergenceThreshold,\n3885-      convergenceMode,\n3886-      depthMode,\n3887-      frontierScope,\n3888-      stopPolicy,\n3889-      reviewFocus,\n3890-      reviewTarget: reviewTargetBinding.target,\n3891-      reviewTargetType: reviewTargetBinding.type,\n3892-    });\n3893-\n3894-    // Sandbox resolution stays write-capable by default even for review lineages:\n3895-    // the review subprocess writes its own iteration artifacts (iterations/iteration-NNN.md,\n3896-    // deep-review-state.jsonl, review-report.md, resource-map.md) INTO lineageDir, so a\n3897-    // blanket read-only default would break those writes. The lineageDir-only write boundary\n3898-    // is enforced by the lineage prompt's explicit write-scope instruction (which also names the\n3899-    // repo tooling a weaker model must not run) rather than by a narrower sandbox; a path-scoped\n3900-    // workspace-write would be the stronger fix if the CLIs exposed one. Callers can still pass an\n3901-    // explicit sandboxMode to override.\n3902-    //\n3903-    // cli-opencode has no OS-level sandbox flag at all, so the generic workspace-write\n3904-    // default would silently ask it to honor a mode it can never enforce. An UNSPECIFIED\n3905-    // sandboxMode for this kind resolves to danger-full-access instead -- the only mode it\n3906-    // can honestly report as effective -- so ordinary dispatch proceeds normally. A caller\n3907-    // that EXPLICITLY asks for read-only/workspace-write still reaches finalizeLineageCommand\n3908-    // unmodified, where that genuine confinement request is rejected.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"765:function readStoredLineageSessionId(loopType, lineageDir) {\n766-  const configName = LINEAGE_CONFIG_BY_LOOP_TYPE[loopType];\n767-  if (!configName) return null;\n768-  let config;\n769-  try {\n770-    config = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(lineageDir, configName), 'utf8'));\n771-  } catch {\n772-    return null;\n773-  }\n774-  if (!config || typeof config !== 'object' || config.status === 'complete') return null;\n775-  const storedId = loopType === 'review'\n776-    ? config.sessionId\n777-    : config.lineage?.sessionId ?? config.sessionId;\n778-  if (typeof storedId !== 'string' || storedId.trim() === '') return null;\n779-  return storedId;\n780-}\n781-\n782-/**\n783- * Artifact directories under this packet owned by a DIFFERENT deep-loop run that is still live.\n784- *\n785- * A run in a sibling phase folder writes its own lock, ledgers, iterations and receipts for the\n786- * whole life of this leaf's dispatch. Those writes land after this leaf's baseline was taken and\n787- * a tree diff cannot tell them from the leaf's own, so blaming this leaf for them is unsound in\n788- * exactly the way a sibling lineage's writes are — with a sharper edge: any of them already in\n789- * HEAD is restored from HEAD, so the guard destroys the live work of a run it never dispatched.\n790- *\n791- * The live lock is the evidence of ownership. A stale lock left behind by a finished run proves\n792- * nothing and grants no exemption (the repo carries many), so a leaf that writes junk into a\n793- * quiet sibling phase folder still fails the iteration exactly as before.\n794- */\n795-async function discoverForeignLiveRunDirs({ specFolder, baseArtifactDir }) {\n--\n388